1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,471
<font color="#000000">This is CABINET,</font>

2
00:00:04,551 --> 00:00:08,522
<font color="#000000">a framework for table question answering published
at ICLR 2024 by researchers at Adobe's</font>

3
00:00:08,602 --> 00:00:12,573
<font color="#000000">MDSR Lab together with IIT Kharagpur and IIT Roorkee.</font>

4
00:00:12,653 --> 00:00:16,624
<font color="#000000">Large language models reason over tables,</font>

5
00:00:16,704 --> 00:00:20,676
<font color="#000000">but only a few cells usually matter for any given
question,</font>

6
00:00:20,756 --> 00:00:24,727
<font color="#000000">and the irrelevant cells act as noise that hurts accuracy.</font>

7
00:00:24,807 --> 00:00:28,778
<font color="#000000">CABINET tackles this by teaching the model to focus
on relevant table content and suppress the rest,</font>

8
00:00:28,858 --> 00:00:32,829
<font color="#000000">rather than deleting parts of the table outright.</font>

9
00:00:32,909 --> 00:00:36,880
<font color="#000000">It sets new state of the art on three challenging
table QA benchmarks.</font>

10
00:00:37,260 --> 00:00:40,383
<font color="#000000">Tables organize information across rows and columns,</font>

11
00:00:40,463 --> 00:00:45,618
<font color="#000000">but for any single question only a small number of
cells actually contain the answer.</font>

12
00:00:45,698 --> 00:00:49,929
<font color="#000000">Everything else is irrelevant to that question and
behaves like noise.</font>

13
00:00:50,009 --> 00:00:54,979
<font color="#000000">Large language models are known to be susceptible
to such distracting information,</font>

14
00:00:55,059 --> 00:00:57,073
<font color="#000000">so their table reasoning degrades,</font>

15
00:00:57,153 --> 00:01:02,493
<font color="#000000">and the problem gets worse as tables grow larger
and carry even more irrelevant content.</font>

16
00:01:02,573 --> 00:01:05,880
<font color="#000000">CABINET is built to address exactly this vulnerability.</font>

17
00:01:06,260 --> 00:01:10,484
<font color="#000000">A natural way to reduce noise is to shrink the
table before answering,</font>

18
00:01:10,564 --> 00:01:15,465
<font color="#000000">and methods like DATER do this by decomposing the
table into a smaller sub-table.</font>

19
00:01:15,545 --> 00:01:21,061
<font color="#000000">The trouble is that hard decomposition is
unforgiving: if the wrong sub-table is extracted,</font>

20
00:01:21,141 --> 00:01:27,148
<font color="#000000">useful information is permanently lost and the
reasoner answers incorrectly with no way to recover.</font>

21
00:01:27,228 --> 00:01:34,035
<font color="#000000">CABINET argues for a softer approach that weighs
relevant parts higher without ever explicitly removing content,</font>

22
00:01:34,115 --> 00:01:40,000
<font color="#000000">so the answering model retains access to the whole
table while being steered toward what matters.</font>

23
00:01:40,380 --> 00:01:44,255
<font color="#000000">The paper contributes CABINET, short for Content
Relevance-Based Noise Reduction.</font>

24
00:01:44,335 --> 00:01:48,210
<font color="#000000">It has two cooperating parts.</font>

25
00:01:48,290 --> 00:01:52,165
<font color="#000000">First,</font>

26
00:01:52,245 --> 00:01:56,120
<font color="#000000">an Unsupervised Relevance Scorer assigns a soft
relevance weight to every table token</font>

27
00:01:56,200 --> 00:02:00,075
<font color="#000000">and is trained differentiably alongside the
question-answering model.</font>

28
00:02:00,155 --> 00:02:04,030
<font color="#000000">Second,</font>

29
00:02:04,110 --> 00:02:07,985
<font color="#000000">a weakly-supervised module generates a parsing
statement describing which rows and columns matter,</font>

30
00:02:08,065 --> 00:02:11,940
<font color="#000000">then highlights the corresponding cells to produce
a cell-based relevance signal.</font>

31
00:02:12,020 --> 00:02:15,895
<font color="#000000">Together they let the model focus without
discarding content,</font>

32
00:02:15,975 --> 00:02:19,850
<font color="#000000">and they deliver new state of the art on three
benchmarks along with stronger robustness to noise and to large tables.</font>

33
00:02:20,230 --> 00:02:22,378
<font color="#000000">CABINET works in a sequence of steps.</font>

34
00:02:22,458 --> 00:02:29,122
<font color="#000000">The table is flattened into a linear string with
header and row markers and embedded together with the question.</font>

35
00:02:29,202 --> 00:02:32,494
<font color="#000000">An Unsupervised Relevance Scorer, a transformer encoder,</font>

36
00:02:32,574 --> 00:02:36,287
<font color="#000000">reads this and predicts a relevance score for each
table token.</font>

37
00:02:36,367 --> 00:02:40,803
<font color="#000000">Because there are no ground-truth annotations for
which cells are relevant,</font>

38
00:02:40,883 --> 00:02:45,741
<font color="#000000">relevance is treated as a latent variable estimated
through variational inference,</font>

39
00:02:45,821 --> 00:02:51,040
<font color="#000000">and the encoder's representation space is shaped by
a clustering loss that groups tokens</font>

40
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:52,906
<font color="#000000">into relevant and non-relevant,</font>

41
00:02:52,986 --> 00:02:56,639
<font color="#000000">a separation loss that pushes the two cluster
centroids apart,</font>

42
00:02:56,719 --> 00:03:02,601
<font color="#000000">and a sparsification loss that drives irrelevant
scores toward zero and relevant scores toward one.</font>

43
00:03:02,681 --> 00:03:06,575
<font color="#000000">Each table token's embedding is multiplied by its
relevance score,</font>

44
00:03:06,655 --> 00:03:10,007
<font color="#000000">so noisy cells are softly suppressed rather than deleted,</font>

45
00:03:10,087 --> 00:03:15,306
<font color="#000000">and the whole system trains end-to-end through the
answer-generation cross-entropy loss.</font>

46
00:03:15,386 --> 00:03:18,918
<font color="#000000">In parallel a weakly-supervised Parsing Statement
Generator,</font>

47
00:03:18,998 --> 00:03:24,097
<font color="#000000">a fine-tuned Flan T5-xl bootstrapped from only
about three hundred manual annotations,</font>

48
00:03:24,177 --> 00:03:28,733
<font color="#000000">writes a natural-language description of which rows
and columns are relevant,</font>

49
00:03:28,813 --> 00:03:32,828
<font color="#000000">and a cell highlighter turns that into a cell-based
relevance score.</font>

50
00:03:32,908 --> 00:03:39,090
<font color="#000000">The unsupervised and cell-based scores are linearly
combined into the final weight applied to the table.</font>

51
00:03:39,470 --> 00:03:44,499
<font color="#000000">CABINET is evaluated on three challenging table
question-answering benchmarks.</font>

52
00:03:44,579 --> 00:03:46,464
<font color="#000000">WikiTableQuestions, or WikiTQ,</font>

53
00:03:46,544 --> 00:03:53,996
<font color="#000000">requires compositional reasoning over tables and
uses short one-to-two-word answers scored by exact-match accuracy.</font>

54
00:03:54,076 --> 00:03:56,877
<font color="#000000">WikiSQL similarly uses exact-match accuracy.</font>

55
00:03:56,957 --> 00:04:00,283
<font color="#000000">FeTaQA asks for long, free-form descriptive answers,</font>

56
00:04:00,363 --> 00:04:02,445
<font color="#000000">which are scored with Sacre-BLEU.</font>

57
00:04:02,525 --> 00:04:08,208
<font color="#000000">The authors additionally release a small dataset of
about three hundred manually written</font>

58
00:04:08,288 --> 00:04:13,710
<font color="#000000">parsing statements used to bootstrap the
weakly-supervised cell-highlighting module.</font>

59
00:04:14,090 --> 00:04:18,547
<font color="#000000">CABINET establishes new state of the art on all
three benchmarks.</font>

60
00:04:18,627 --> 00:04:23,502
<font color="#000000">On WikiTQ it reaches sixty-nine point one percent
exact-match accuracy,</font>

61
00:04:23,582 --> 00:04:29,086
<font color="#000000">outperforming the strongest baseline in each
category, including OmniTab, DATER,</font>

62
00:04:29,166 --> 00:04:33,344
<font color="#000000">and fine-tuned Flan T5-xl by six point four, three
point two,</font>

63
00:04:33,424 --> 00:04:36,694
<font color="#000000">and four point seven absolute percentage points,</font>

64
00:04:36,774 --> 00:04:41,929
<font color="#000000">and it beats much larger GPT-3 and Codex based
in-context learning methods.</font>

65
00:04:42,009 --> 00:04:45,768
<font color="#000000">On FeTaQA it achieves a Sacre-BLEU of forty point five,</font>

66
00:04:45,848 --> 00:04:50,375
<font color="#000000">and on WikiSQL it reaches eighty-nine point five
percent accuracy,</font>

67
00:04:50,455 --> 00:04:52,538
<font color="#000000">pushing past the previous best.</font>

68
00:04:52,618 --> 00:04:58,820
<font color="#000000">Notably all of this is achieved with a compact five
hundred sixty million parameter model.</font>

69
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:01,936
<font color="#000000">Ablations confirm both components are needed.</font>

70
00:05:02,016 --> 00:05:06,941
<font color="#000000">For the Unsupervised Relevance Scorer, applying
clustering, centroid-separation,</font>

71
00:05:07,021 --> 00:05:13,949
<font color="#000000">and sparsification losses together lifts WikiTQ
accuracy from sixty point eight to sixty-five point six percent,</font>

72
00:05:14,029 --> 00:05:16,452
<font color="#000000">whereas any subset gives little benefit,</font>

73
00:05:16,532 --> 00:05:19,580
<font color="#000000">showing the three losses only help in combination.</font>

74
00:05:19,660 --> 00:05:21,457
<font color="#000000">For the two relevance signals,</font>

75
00:05:21,537 --> 00:05:26,775
<font color="#000000">fusing the unsupervised score at weight zero point
seven with the cell-based score at</font>

76
00:05:26,855 --> 00:05:28,965
<font color="#000000">weight zero point three is optimal,</font>

77
00:05:29,045 --> 00:05:31,843
<font color="#000000">giving sixty-nine point one percent on WikiTQ.</font>

78
00:05:31,923 --> 00:05:37,600
<font color="#000000">Relying on the cell-based signal alone collapses
accuracy to thirty-seven point six percent,</font>

79
00:05:37,680 --> 00:05:44,670
<font color="#000000">confirming the unsupervised scorer is the primary
driver and the parsing-statement module is a complementary aid.</font>

80
00:05:45,050 --> 00:05:49,714
<font color="#000000">The headline numbers are sixty-nine point one
percent accuracy on WikiTQ,</font>

81
00:05:49,794 --> 00:05:52,509
<font color="#000000">a Sacre-BLEU of forty point five on FeTaQA,</font>

82
00:05:52,589 --> 00:05:56,083
<font color="#000000">and eighty-nine point five percent accuracy on WikiSQL,</font>

83
00:05:56,163 --> 00:06:01,608
<font color="#000000">each a new state of the art achieved with just five
hundred sixty million parameters.</font>

84
00:06:01,688 --> 00:06:02,908
<font color="#000000">Beyond raw accuracy,</font>

85
00:06:02,988 --> 00:06:08,952
<font color="#000000">CABINET is markedly more robust: under table
perturbations such as row and column permutation</font>

86
00:06:09,032 --> 00:06:12,656
<font color="#000000">and cell replacement it degrades far less than baselines,</font>

87
00:06:12,736 --> 00:06:18,505
<font color="#000000">with roughly an eleven and a half percent smaller
performance drop than OmniTab on WikiTQ,</font>

88
00:06:18,585 --> 00:06:21,690
<font color="#000000">and it holds its advantage as tables grow larger.</font>

89
00:06:22,070 --> 00:06:27,895
<font color="#FFFFFF">The core lesson of CABINET is that you do not need
to cut a table down to answer questions about it.</font>

90
00:06:27,975 --> 00:06:32,028
<font color="#FFFFFF">By softly weighting every cell according to a
learned relevance score,</font>

91
00:06:32,108 --> 00:06:36,812
<font color="#FFFFFF">instead of hard-decomposing the table and risking
the loss of useful information,</font>

92
00:06:36,892 --> 00:06:41,536
<font color="#FFFFFF">the model keeps full access to the data while being
steered toward what matters.</font>

93
00:06:41,616 --> 00:06:47,263
<font color="#FFFFFF">Pairing a differentiable unsupervised relevance
scorer with a weakly-supervised parsing-statement</font>

94
00:06:47,343 --> 00:06:51,810
<font color="#FFFFFF">cell highlighter yields both higher accuracy and
greater robustness to noise,</font>

95
00:06:51,890 --> 00:06:58,660
<font color="#FFFFFF">setting new state of the art on three table QA
benchmarks with a compact five hundred sixty million parameter model.</font>
